# Build output. The scanner is assembled by scripts/package.sh into shellsight/bin/ (windows) or
# shellsight/dist/ (linux), and the release archives land in shellsight/release/.
#
# ANCHORED, deliberately. A bare `dist/` matches any directory of that name at any depth, which
# includes console/internal/web/dist -- the built dashboard that MUST stay tracked for web.go's
# //go:embed to find it. That is not hypothetical: the unanchored version of this file silently
# re-ignored the dashboard, and the export self-test caught it by asking git what it actually
# ignores rather than reading this comment.
shellsight/bin/
shellsight/dist/
shellsight/release/

# The YARA engine is fetched, not vendored: scripts/fetch-yara-x.sh
third_party/

# Go, .NET and Node build residue
*.exe
*.test
*.o
*.obj
__pycache__/
node_modules/
probes/dotnetmem/bin/
probes/dotnetmem/obj/
probes/javamem/out/
probes/javamem/test-out/

# Scan output. Every run writes report.json, findings.ndjson and summary.txt INSIDE a run folder, so
# ignoring the folder is sufficient.
#
# Bare `report.json` etc. are deliberately NOT listed. They looked harmless and were not: a bare
# rule swallowed console/internal/report/testdata/report.json, a tracked test fixture, so a fresh
# clone would have failed internal/report's tests with a file that was present but untracked. Same
# lesson as the anchored dist/ rule above -- an unanchored filename matches at every depth.
run_*/

# NOT ignored, deliberately: console/internal/web/dist. internal/web/web.go embeds it with
# `//go:embed all:dist`, so ignoring it would make a clone serve placeholder.html instead of the
# dashboard. It is also pinned -text in .gitattributes, because it is Vite's bytes rather than
# source and a line-ending rewrite would change what gets embedded.
